[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Elasticsearch Snapshots, Backup, and Disaster Recovery Tutorial"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"Data protection is a critical aspect of maintaining any production Elasticsearch cluster. This technical tutorial covers how to configure snapshot repositories, perform manual and automated backups, and execute a disaster recovery plan."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"Prerequisites and Repository Configuration"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"Before taking snapshots, you must register a snapshot repository. Elasticsearch supports various repository types, including shared file systems, AWS S3, Google Cloud Storage, and Azure Blob Storage."},{"order":5,"type":"code","text":"PUT _snapshot/my_backup_repository
{
  "type": "fs",
  "settings": {
    "compress": true,
    "location": "/mount/backups"
  }
}"},{"order":6,"type":"subheading","text":"Creating Snapshots"},{"order":7,"type":"paragraph","text":"A snapshot is a backup of a running Elasticsearch cluster. You can back up the entire cluster, specific indices, or exclude certain data. Snapshots are incremental, meaning they only store data that has changed since the previous snapshot."},{"order":8,"type":"code","text":"PUT _snapshot/my_backup_repository/snapshot_1?wait_for_completion=true
{
  "indices": "logs-*",
  "ignore_unavailable": true,
  "include_global_state": false
}"},{"order":9,"type":"subheading","text":"Automating Backups with SLM"},{"order":10,"type":"paragraph","text":"Manual snapshots are useful for maintenance, but production environments require automation. Snapshot Lifecycle Management (SLM) allows you to define policies to automatically create and delete snapshots."},{"order":11,"type":"code","text":"PUT _slm/policy/daily-backup-policy
{
  "schedule": "0 30 2 * * ?",
  "name": "backup-<now{YYYY.MM.DD}>",
  "repository": "my_backup_repository",
  "config": {
    "indices": ["*"],
    "include_global_state": true
  },
  "retention": {
    "expire_after": "30d",
    "min_count": 5,
    "max_count": 50
  }
}"},{"order":12,"type":"subheading","text":"Disaster Recovery and Restoration"},{"order":13,"type":"paragraph","text":"In the event of catastrophic data loss, you can restore indices or the entire cluster state from your snapshot repository. Ensure the cluster is healthy before initiating a restore operation."},{"order":14,"type":"code","text":"POST _snapshot/my_backup_repository/snapshot_1/_restore
{
  "indices": "logs-2023.10.15",
  "ignore_unavailable": true,
  "include_global_state": false,
  "rename_pattern": "logs-(.+)",
  "rename_replacement": "restored_logs-$1"
}"},{"order":15,"type":"subheading","text":"Best Practices for Elasticsearch DR"},{"order":16,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Always store snapshots in an external, highly available remote storage system (such as S3) separate from your cluster nodes."},{"order":17,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Test your restore process regularly in a staging environment to ensure your backups are valid and recoverable."},{"order":18,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Monitor snapshot health using Elasticsearch monitoring APIs and set up alerts for failed SLM policies."}]
